<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashur (god)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashur was the chief god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, originally the deity of the city of Ashur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, with which he shared his name. As Assyria grew into an empire he became the supreme god of the state, and kings presented their wars as campaigns on his behalf; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>'s letter to Ashur reports the campaign of 714 BCE against Urartu. His cult faded with the fall of Assyria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashur-god.jpg" alt="Ashur (god)" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Levi Clancy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neo-Assyrian_archaeological_Halamata_reliefs_overlooking_Nohadra,_modern-day_Duhok,_Kurdistan_Region_of_Iraq_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
